  },
  "question": {
    "text": "The number of ways,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>16</mn></math> identical cubes, of which&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>11</mn></math> are blue and rest are red, can be placed in a row so that between any two red cubes there should be at least&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> blue cubes, is ______."
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "56",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Given,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>&nbsp;blue&nbsp;&amp; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math>&nbsp;red spherical&nbsp;ball</p><p>Now according to&nbsp;question&nbsp;there should be minimum 2 blue ball in between any two red ball.</p><p>So assume&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></math>&nbsp;are&nbsp;number of blue balls,</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0101_d9cb2a2cb87e.png\" style=\"width: 311px; height: 40px;\" /></p><p>So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>&#160;</mo></math>&nbsp;......(i)</p><p>Where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>&#10878;</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p><p>Now let&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p><p>So equation (i) becomes</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p><p>Now total ways will be</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></msup><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><msup><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></msup><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>56</mn></math></p>"
  }
}